  "query_preview": "..\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Very warm to hot high temperatures in the upper\n80s to low 90s possible Monday-Wednesday. Highest apparent \ntemperatures Tuesday for all but Downeast. Downeast, especially \ncoastal areas should see the highest apparent temperatures \nWednesday.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1 DESCRIPTION...\nCurrent guidance is showing high confidence in a warming trend\nbeginning on Monday and continuing through the middle of next\nweek. Temperatures are currently expected to peak Tuesday across\nall inland areas and Wednesday for coastal Downeast. Highs\nshould reach the upper 80s over the North and the low 90s \nfurther south. Moderate humidity with dew points in the 60s are \nexpected which should push the apparent temperature higher. A \ncooldown is likely through the end of the week as cooler air \nmoves in behind a cold front expected to exit off the coast on \nWednesday.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...There is a chance for strong thunderstorms area \nwide on Tuesday, and mainly across coastal Downeast Maine on \nWednesday.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2 DESCRIPTION...\nThe Storm Prediction Center has placed all but coastal Maine in\na 15% risk for severe thunderstorms on Tuesday. Current \nensemble guidance is expressing good agreement on a strong \nupper-level disturbance approaching our area on Tuesday and \npassing through the area Tuesday night with a cold front at the \nsurface. High surface temperatures and ample moisture should \nfuel considerable instability as ensembles are showing around \n1000 to 1500 J/kg. A jet of strong upper-level winds is expected\nto contribute to deep-layer shear of around 50 kts which is \nquite high for this time of year. Both daytime and nocturnal \nstrong thunderstorms are currently possible with the setup.\nWhile the initial threat is for northern and central areas\nTuesday, a secondary risk may be possible Wednesday Downeast if\nthe cold front doesn't clear out of the area by then. This is \nfar out so there still exists some uncertainty, especially as to\nthe timing of the cold frontal passage which will determine \nwhen and where storms might occur and how severe storms can \nbecome. However, it is certainly worth keeping a close eye on \ngiven the agreement in the guidance.",
